==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|offenen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|offanōn}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*upanōną}}. Compare {{cog|nl|openen}} and English {{m|en|open}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʔœfnən/|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|öff|nen|lang=de}}
* {{a|Germany}} {{audio|De-öffnen.ogg|lang=de}}
* {{a|Austria}} {{audio|De-at-öffnen.ogg|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|öffnet|öffnete|geöffnet}}

# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[open]] {{gloss|to make something accessible or allow for passage by moving from a shut position}}
#: {{ux|de|Das Kind '''öffnete''' die Fenster.|t=The child '''opened''' the windows.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to open {{gloss|to make accessible to customers or clients}}
# {{lb|de|transitive|computing}} to open {{gloss|to load into memory for viewing or editing}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to open, [[get]], or [[answer]] the door
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to open {{gloss|to become open}}
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to [[open up]] (to), [[confide]] (in) {{gloss|to reveal oneself; share personal information about oneself}}

====Usage notes====
While '''''öffnen''''' isn't explicitly formal, it is fairly infrequent in everyday spoken language, typically replaced by the colloquial {{m|de|aufmachen}}.

